% How Deep Is Your Data? Estimating Recursive Generation Depth via Statistical Fingerprints
% Target: EMNLP 2026 (ARR May cycle)
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[review]{acl}
\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage{subcaption}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}

\title{How Deep Is Your Data? Estimating Recursive Generation Depth\\via Statistical Fingerprints}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{abstract}

\input{introduction}

\input{related_work}

\input{method}

\input{atlas}

\input{generalization}

\input{discussion}

\input{conclusion}

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{section_appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating four axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and end-to-end learning (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares classification methods on the Pythia-1.4B nucleus setting.
Three feature-based methods---Random Forest (RF), CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020}, and DivEye \cite{diveye2026}---all operate on the same 19 statistical features and achieve statistically indistinguishable accuracy (78.1--78.2\%; pairwise bootstrap $p>0.05$ after Bonferroni correction, 95\% CI within $\pm$1.1pp).
These three significantly outperform CE-MLP ($p<0.001$, $+$3.8pp), which in turn trails logistic regression by $\sim$0.1pp.
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%), confirming that depth estimation requires the multi-dimensional feature representation defined in \S\ref{sec:method}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Comparison of classification methods on Pythia-1.4B nucleus. RF, CORAL, and DivEye use the same 19 statistical features and are statistically indistinguishable (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected). Chance = 25\%. 5-fold CV std: 0.2--0.9pp across all methods.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Method & 4-class Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
RF (19 features) & 78.2 \\
CORAL & 78.1 \\
DivEye & 78.2 \\
CE-MLP & 74.9 \\
LR & 75.0 \\
DeepSeek V3.2 (zero-shot)\footnotemark & 36.2 \\
ppl-regression (single feature) & 25.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\footnotetext{Evaluated on 500 samples (125 per class) from the Pythia-1.4B nucleus cell only, due to API cost; 95\% CI: $[32.0\%, 40.4\%]$. All other methods use the full 20{,}000-sample set.}
\end{table}

Identical performance across three structurally different classifiers confirms the signal resides in the features, not the architecture; a zero-shot LLM (DeepSeek V3.2) reaches only 36.2\%.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89.5\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%), with d2 recall ranging from 3.7\% to 19\%---strategy-induced shifts pose a greater challenge than architectural differences.

\paragraph{Temperature-induced boundary inversion.}
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5; \S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).
Per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers the boundary but leaves a residual gap to within-strategy performance (per-model details in Limitations).

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:e2e}

DeBERTa-v3-base \cite{he2021_deberta_v3} and RoBERTa-base \cite{liu2019_roberta} finetuned end-to-end both converge to ${\sim}$59\% (19pp below RF 78.2\%; 1.2pp inter-backbone gap rules out architecture bias).
SHAP TreeExplainer shows perplexity and surprisal features account for 88.8\% of RF's contribution; three importance measures achieve strong concordance (Kendall's $W{=}0.90$, $p{<}0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}), while linear probing from DeBERTa hidden states yields negative $R^2$ for \textsc{var\_surprisal} and \textsc{p90\_ppl} (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap}).
DCOL \cite{cloc2025} reached 64.5\% (Appendix~\ref{app:dcol_iterations}); all end-to-end approaches (58--65\%) underperform feature-based methods, suggesting depth estimation benefits from explicit tail statistics absent from PLM representations.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Cross-strategy deployment constraint.}
Cross-strategy transfer induces a d1/d2 boundary inversion under temperature sampling.
Per-strategy z-score normalization partially corrects the inversion (GPT-2~XL d1v2 AUC $0.24{\to}0.56$, OLMo $0.19{\to}0.70$) but leaves a residual gap relative to within-strategy performance (${\sim}0.83$), indicating that a two-stage pipeline (strategy identification + strategy-conditioned classification) is needed for reliable cross-strategy deployment.

\paragraph{d0 inflation of headline metrics.}
Depth~0 (human-written text) is near-perfectly separable (recall $>$98\% across all settings), inflating headline accuracy.
Cross-model transfer accuracy of 66.4\% is substantially driven by d0; d2 recall drops to 26.8\% in the worst case (Pythia holdout).

\paragraph{Controlled vs.\ ecological setting.}
All experiments use Wikipedia seed text with controlled recursive generation (LoRA fine-tuning, 1--1.5B parameter models).
While depth signal is the dominant classification factor (5-fold CV 59--82\%, 3.4$\times$ chance), a residual domain signal from seed-article stylistic differences exists (${\sim}$2$\times$ chance conditional on seed group; LOGO accuracy drops 8pp on average; Appendix~\ref{app:domain_bias}).
In the most affected cell (Pythia top-$k$), individual LOGO folds drop to 22.9\%, approaching chance.
Transfer to mixed-domain corpora, instruction-tuned models, and larger scales (7B+) remains open.
Real-world documents may also interleave segments at different recursion depths; extending to segment-level estimation introduces a granularity--statistical-power tradeoff.

\paragraph{Adversarial robustness.}
Adversarial paraphrasing \cite{sadasivan2023_paraphrase} could disrupt the perplexity tail features that drive classification; depth estimation's robustness under such attacks is untested.

\paragraph{EDD monotonicity assumption.}
Our EDD definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}) requires all pairwise AUCs up to depth $d^*$ to exceed $T$, which is well-behaved when AUC decays monotonically---as observed in all our configurations.
However, \citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} show that early recursion can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality, which could produce non-monotonic AUC profiles (e.g., a dip at d1/d2 followed by recovery at d2/d3).
Our cross-strategy transfer results provide an empirical example: training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature yields d1-vs-d2 AUC as low as 0.129 (GPT-2~XL) and 0.253 (OLMo), violating the monotonicity assumption.
In such cases EDD would conservatively report a lower value than the deepest discriminable pair, potentially underestimating the true discriminable range; extending EDD to handle non-contiguous discriminable regions is future work.
The choice of threshold $T$ is also application-dependent: at $T{=}0.80$, several cells lose one depth level relative to $T{=}0.75$.

\paragraph{Reference model dependency.}
Surprisal-based features are computed relative to GPT-2 base (124M), introducing partial overlap when GPT-2~XL is held out, though GPT-2~XL holdout accuracy (67.6\%) does not exceed the unrelated OLMo (69.2\%).
The choice of reference model affects feature values; a different reference model may shift absolute feature magnitudes and potentially alter discriminability at depth boundaries.

\paragraph{Additional caveats.}
Detection features degrade under domain shift \cite{pudasaini2026_detection_fails}; our depth features are likely susceptible.
\citet{falahati2026_curated} show that curated synthetic data need not trigger collapse, attenuating the fingerprints we exploit.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses publicly available pretrained language models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2~XL) and Wikipedia text as seed data.
No human subjects were involved, no private or sensitive data were collected, and no new models were released.
Our method is intended to support data provenance and training-data auditing; we acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be misused to circumvent content moderation, though such risks are shared with all detection-oriented research.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 69.6\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.4\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature), all exceeding the 25\% chance baseline by a wide margin.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (76.6--85.4\%), followed by OLMo (74.2--82.0\%) and Pythia (69.6--78.2\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 8.4~pp, comparable to the 7.1~pp mean within-strategy model gap---decoding strategy and model family contribute roughly equally to performance variance.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Four-class RF accuracy (\%) across the $3 \times 3$ atlas, with d1--d3 three-class accuracy in parentheses (chance = 25\% / 33\%). Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all cells.}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
& Nucleus & Temp.\ $\tau{=}0.9$ & Top-$k$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B  & 78.2\,(71.2) & 76.3\,(70.6) & 69.6\,(58.8) \\
OLMo-1B     & 78.8\,(75.0) & 82.0\,(76.3) & 74.2\,(66.1) \\
GPT-2~XL    & 83.3\,(79.0) & 85.4\,(82.6) & 76.6\,(69.5) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Universality}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Despite the 15.8~pp accuracy spread across cells, a small feature family dominates in every setting.
The 90th-percentile perplexity (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) ranks top-2 by Gini importance in all 9 cells; surprisal variance (\texttt{var\_surprisal}) in 7/9 (8/9 under SHAP; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}).
Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance} shows the full per-cell ranking.

Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$ (range $[0.619, 0.954]$): models agree on which features carry depth information even when they differ in absolute accuracy.
Among strategies, top-$k$ yields the highest cross-model rank correlation ($\rho = 0.811$)---the deterministic vocabulary truncation imposes a more uniform feature landscape across architectures, at the cost of weaker overall signal (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).

Percentile-based features outperform their moment-based counterparts.
Token-level perplexity distributions are heavy-tailed: individual outlier passages can dominate the arithmetic mean of their depth class, whereas \texttt{p90\_ppl} is robust to such extremes while still capturing the tail erosion that signals recursive depth.

The feature hierarchy also shifts with depth.
At shallow boundaries (d1 vs.\ d2), \texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates importance; at deeper boundaries (d3 vs.\ d4, d4 vs.\ d5), tail perplexity statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) take over.
This transition aligns with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursion narrows the surprisal distribution (captured by its variance), while later steps erode the perplexity tail (captured by upper quantiles and kurtosis).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top-5 features by Gini importance. \textbf{Bold}: top-2. \texttt{p90\_ppl} top-2 in 9/9; \texttt{var\_surprisal} 7/9 (8/9 under SHAP).}
\label{tab:feature_importance}
% [Populate with per-cell top-5 feature rankings from importance analysis.]
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Cell & Top-5 features (ranked) \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{@{}l}{\textit{[9-row table; see supplementary materials for importance scores]}} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
% \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/atlas_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Atlas feature importance heatmap. Rows: 19 features grouped by category (\S\ref{sec:method:features}); columns: 9 model--strategy cells. Color intensity encodes RF Gini importance. The perplexity-tail and surprisal-variance blocks maintain high importance across all cells.}
\label{fig:atlas_heatmap}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Monotonicity and Inter-Depth Distances}
\label{sec:atlas:monotonicity}

Depth features exhibit strong monotonic trends.
Spearman correlations between cell-level feature medians and depth $d$ range from $\rho = -0.82$ to $-0.89$ for perplexity-family features across all 9 cells.
Two complementary signal groups drive this structure.
Perplexity tail statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{mean\_surprisal}) decrease monotonically with depth ($\rho = -1.0$ across cell-level medians), reflecting the narrowing of the token-predictability distribution under recursive finetuning.
Repetition features (\texttt{rep\_2gram}, \texttt{rep\_3gram}, \texttt{rep\_4gram}) increase monotonically, capturing the growing self-repetition that accompanies distribution collapse.

Inter-depth distances are non-uniform.
Binary d0-vs-d1 AUC exceeds 0.99 in all 9 cells, whereas d2-vs-d3 AUC ranges from 0.69 to 0.89.
The $d{=}0 \to d{=}1$ gap reflects the qualitative jump from human-written to machine-generated text; subsequent recursion steps produce progressively smaller distributional shifts.
This non-uniformity has practical implications: distinguishing real from first-generation synthetic text is near-trivial, but separating adjacent deep generations requires increasingly sensitive features---precisely the tail statistics identified in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
% \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/feature_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Feature trajectories across depths $d{=}0$ to $d{=}3$, stratified by model (columns) and strategy (line style). Top row: \texttt{p90\_ppl} (monotonically decreasing). Middle: \texttt{var\_surprisal} (decreasing). Bottom: \texttt{rep\_3gram} (increasing). Shaded bands: interquartile range. The $d{=}0 \to d{=}1$ gap exceeds all subsequent inter-depth gaps.}
\label{fig:feature_trajectory}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{EDD Analysis}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

We extend the benchmark to depth $d{=}5$ for all three models under nucleus sampling.
Each model's depth-3 LoRA checkpoint is used to finetune and generate depth-4 data, and the process repeats for depth~5; feature extraction uses the same reference model (GPT-2 base, 124M).
We train dedicated binary RF classifiers (500 trees) for each adjacent pair, as defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}.

\paragraph{AUC decay profile.}
Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} plots the binary pairwise AUC decay curves.
For Pythia-1.4B (nucleus), AUC decays monotonically: d0-vs-d1 $= 0.999$, d1-vs-d2 $= 0.925$, d2-vs-d3 $= 0.823$, d3-vs-d4 $= 0.784$, d4-vs-d5 $= 0.683$.
The steepest drop occurs between the d1-vs-d2 and d2-vs-d3 pairs; the curve then flattens as the system approaches an indistinguishable regime gradually rather than crossing a sharp boundary.

\paragraph{Cross-model convergence at depth 4--5.}
All three models exhibit a consistent profile at the deep boundaries.
The d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters near the $T{=}0.75$ threshold: Pythia $0.784$ (bootstrap 95\% CI $[0.765, 0.802]$, $p = 0.001$), OLMo $0.765$ ($[0.744, 0.785]$, $p = 0.091$), GPT-2~XL $0.750$ ($[0.728, 0.771]$, $p = 0.505$), where $p$ denotes the one-tailed bootstrap probability under $H_0\!: \mathrm{AUC} \leq 0.75$ (1{,}000 iterations).
Only Pythia achieves significance at conventional levels; OLMo is marginal and GPT-2~XL falls on the threshold despite its higher in-distribution accuracy.
The d4-vs-d5 AUC falls uniformly below 0.70 across all three models (Pythia $0.683$, OLMo $0.684$, GPT-2~XL $0.681$), confirming a model-independent signal saturation point at depth~5.

This pattern---directional consistency across models with graded statistical strength---supports interpreting the d3--d4 region as a \emph{transition zone} rather than a binary boundary.
The continuous AUC decay curve provides a more informative characterization of depth discriminability than any single $\mathrm{EDD}_T$ value.

\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity} reports $\mathrm{EDD}_T$ across the primary 9-cell benchmark (depths 0--3) at four threshold values.
At $T{=}0.65$, all 9 cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD} = 3$; at $T{=}0.75$, 7 of 9 cells maintain $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$; at $T{=}0.80$, the count drops to 5 of 9.
The cells losing $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$ first are consistently the top-$k$ configurations (Pythia top-$k$ at $T{=}0.70$, then OLMo top-$k$ at $T{=}0.75$), where the d2-vs-d3 pair is the consistent bottleneck across all 9 cells.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Number of atlas cells achieving $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$ at each threshold $T$, across 9 model--strategy cells (depths 0--3). The d2-vs-d3 boundary is the bottleneck; top-$k$ cells drop first.}
\label{tab:edd_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
& $T{=}0.65$ & $T{=}0.70$ & $T{=}0.75$ & $T{=}0.80$ \\
\midrule
Cells with $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$ & 9/9 & 8/9 & 7/9 & 5/9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
% \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Binary pairwise AUC decay curves for three models (nucleus sampling, depths 0--5). Horizontal dashed lines mark $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$. All three curves converge below 0.70 at d4-vs-d5, while d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters in the range 0.750--0.784. Shaded bands: bootstrap 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Decision boundary dilution.}
\label{sec:atlas:edd:dilution}
EDD's binary-pair design is motivated by \emph{decision boundary dilution}: when a multi-class classifier must simultaneously separate $K$ depths, its decision boundaries are shared across all $\binom{K}{2}$ class pairs, diluting the capacity allocated to any single adjacent pair.
Concretely, in the Pythia nucleus setting, the d2\,vs.\,d3 pairwise AUC degrades from 0.804 in a 4-class pipeline to 0.689 in a 6-class pipeline---a 14.3\% relative decline---despite identical underlying data.
Mean classification accuracy likewise drops from 99.5\% (binary) to 78.3\% (4-class) to 62.9\% (6-class).
Because the pairwise signal between depths $d$ and $d{+}1$ does not change when unrelated depths are added, this degradation is entirely attributable to boundary dilution, not signal loss.
Binary pairwise classifiers eliminate this confound, isolating the true adjacent-pair discriminability that EDD is designed to measure.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Trajectories}
\label{sec:atlas:strategy}

The three decoding strategies induce qualitatively distinct degradation trajectories that resist reduction to a binary taxonomy.

\paragraph{Nucleus ($p{=}0.95$): stable baseline.}
Nucleus sampling produces the most consistent accuracy across models (78.2--83.3\%), with moderate AUC decay and no anomalous pairwise boundaries.
It serves as the reference trajectory against which temperature and top-$k$ effects are measured.

\paragraph{Temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$): signal enhancement with boundary reversal.}
Temperature sampling yields the highest accuracy in 2 of 3 models (GPT-2~XL $85.4\%$, OLMo $82.0\%$) and the second-highest in Pythia ($76.3\%$).
Unlike nucleus and top-$k$, which truncate the vocabulary, temperature uniformly scales logits, redistributing probability mass across all tokens.
This accelerates convergence to an attractor distribution during recursive finetuning~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}, amplifying the statistical fingerprint at each depth step.
The acceleration manifests as a systematic depth shift: temperature depth-$N$ features approximate nucleus/top-$k$ depth-$(N{+}1)$ features.
For GPT-2~XL, temperature depth-1 \texttt{p90\_ppl} median is 92.2, falling within the nucleus/top-$k$ depth-2 range of 65--92.

This depth shift creates a boundary reversal risk in cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:generalization}): a classifier trained on nucleus and top-$k$ data systematically overpredicts depth for temperature-generated text, because temperature depth-1 features overlap with nucleus/top-$k$ depth-2 features.

\paragraph{Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$): signal attenuation, direction preserved.}
Top-$k$ sampling produces the lowest accuracy in every model (69.6--76.6\%), with a mean 8.4~pp gap from the strongest strategy.
Hard vocabulary truncation removes low-probability tokens that carry depth-discriminative information, attenuating the overall signal.
The \emph{direction} of feature evolution, however, is preserved: perplexity statistics still decrease monotonically with depth, and feature importance rankings maintain the highest cross-model correlation ($\rho = 0.811$).
Top-$k$ compresses the depth signal without distorting it.

\paragraph{Strategy vs.\ model effect.}
The strategy effect (8.4~pp range) exceeds the model effect (7.1~pp range) across the atlas, indicating that deployment-time knowledge of the decoding strategy is at least as important as knowledge of the generator model for accurate depth estimation.

